# Plain-language closings

Every reply to the user must end with a short wrap-up in everyday English.
The wrap-up is for someone who did not read the jargon, step numbers, or file names.

## What to do

- Close with **In short:** (or the same idea in other words).
- Say what happened, why it matters, and what is still true. One idea per sentence.
- Prefer people and actions: "the auto installer copied a folder the guide said to leave alone."
- Do not use stacked nouns, class-of-bug labels, or insider shorthand as the last word.

## What not to do

```
# BAD — last paragraph is only for people already in the thread
Bottom line: the generator keeps turning guide constraints into Move folder\*,
or it binds the delete to Override and runs it before the files exist.
Holo and Bio stay in lockstep with each other and out of lockstep with manual.
```

```
# GOOD — last paragraph a new reader can use
In short: both auto installs followed a machine-written recipe, not the human
guide. The recipe copied extra files the guide said to skip, and it tried to
delete some files before they were there. That is why the two auto trees match
each other and still do not match the manual tree.
```

## When the rest of the message is technical

Keep the evidence. Do not dumb down the body. Only the closing must stand alone.
